WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.496
Diagnostics: is the network healthy?

2
00:00:02.776 --> 00:00:06.296
The second half of the lecture builds the habit of looking inside.

3
00:00:06.576 --> 00:00:14.427
Four plots, recomputed as training runs: Forward activations per layer: their distribution and the fraction near saturation.

4
00:00:14.707 --> 00:00:19.805
Healthy layers have similar spreads; a layer piled up at the extremes is saturating.

5
00:00:20.085 --> 00:00:24.416
Backward gradients per layer: their distribution should be similar across layers.

6
00:00:24.696 --> 00:00:29.261
Shrinking toward the input means vanishing gradients; growing means exploding.

7
00:00:29.541 --> 00:00:35.813
Parameter gradients, especially the ratio of gradient spread to parameter spread for each weight matrix.

8
00:00:36.093 --> 00:00:42.515
One layer with a wildly different ratio (the lecture finds one) will train at a different speed from the rest.

9
00:00:42.795 --> 00:00:55.509
Update-to-data ratio over time: for each parameter tensor, the standard deviation of the update (learning rate times gradient) divided by the standard deviation of the parameter, on a log scale.

10
00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:58.819
It says how fast each tensor is actually changing.

11
00:00:59.099 --> 00:01:08.635
Watch the lecture’s discussion at 01:39:55 for the rough target he uses and what it means when a layer sits far above or below it.

12
00:01:08.915 --> 00:01:25.107
The fully linear experiment at 01:32:07 is a nice piece of math made visible: without nonlinearities the whole network collapses to one linear map, yet the training dynamics still depend on initialization scale, because the gradients do.
